{"type": "say", "text": "Hi everyone, thanks for coming. As most of you know, Tariq asked about using my backyard greenhouse for a small seedling sale one weekend in June. The space is available, and it's got worktables, a water tap, and a side entrance from the alley that would work well for customer flow. I'll be making the final call on whether this goes ahead and on what terms. Before I decide, I'd like to hear from everyone — what terms you're proposing, what other options might be on the table, and any practical setup concerns. Let's start with you, Tariq — what are you thinking?"}
```

```json
{"type": "say", "text": "Thanks, Elena. Here's what I'm thinking: I'd like to use the greenhouse on Saturday, June 22, from 9 AM to 3 PM. That gives a solid six-hour window for customers to come through the side entrance without crowding the alley. In return, I'd offer you 40 percent of the gross seedling proceeds for the day. I know that's a meaningful cut, but I want to make sure you feel fairly compensated for the space. On top of that, I'd commit to two greenhouse-maintenance visits in November — whatever you need most, whether that's cleaning, minor repairs, or winterizing. The worktables and water tap are perfect for potting demos and keeping the seedlings hydrated throughout the day. One practical concern I have: I'd want to confirm how many customers we might reasonably expect so I can plan layout and signage for the alley entrance. I don't have another venue lined up, so this greenhouse is really my top choice right now."}
```

```json
{"type": "say", "text": "Hi Elena, thanks for hearing from everyone. I actually have an alternative offer to put on the table. I'm interested in using the greenhouse that same Saturday — June 22 — for a plant sale of my own. I'd like to propose a flat $180 payment to you for use of the space for the day. That way you'd have guaranteed compensation regardless of how sales go, and it's a straightforward arrangement. The worktables, water tap, and side entrance would work just as well for my setup. I don't have specific customer volume estimates yet, but I'd plan to keep alley traffic manageable. I know Tariq's offer includes a revenue share plus maintenance visits, so I understand you'll want to weigh both. I wanted to make sure you had my proposal before you decide."}
```

```json
{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF3", "text": "I've got a practical setup concern I think everyone needs to hear. I actually measured the greenhouse floor recently, and it safely supports at most six filled water trays. Whatever sale ends up happening — whether it's Tariq's or Maya's — the setup needs to keep all six or fewer trays on the raised benches, not on the floor. That's a hard limit Elena should factor into her decision, and both of you should plan your layout around it."}
```

```json
{"type": "say", "text": "Thanks for those fair questions, Elena. On customer volume — honestly, I don't have a firm estimate yet, which is why I raised it. For a neighborhood seedling sale on a Saturday morning, I'd guess somewhere in the range of 20 to 40 people over the six hours, but I'd want to keep signage clear so folks don't linger and block the alley. On water trays — given Jon's hard limit of six on the raised benches, I'd plan for five trays max. That leaves a buffer and keeps everything off the floor. I'd arrange them on the worktables with the tap nearby for top-ups. Regarding a minimum on the revenue share — yes, I'd be willing to commit to a floor. If 40 percent of gross comes out to less than $120, I'd guarantee at least $120 for you for the day. That way you're not taking on all the downside risk if foot traffic is light. I'd still keep the two November maintenance visits as part of the package. I think that rounds out my proposal — Saturday June 22, 9 to 3, 40 percent of gross with a $120 minimum, five trays max on the benches, and two maintenance visits in November."}
```

```json
{"type": "say", "text": "Thanks for the fair hearing, Elena. I appreciate you giving me the chance to respond. Yes, I'm willing to raise my minimum guarantee from $120 to $150. That way you have a stronger floor — closer to Maya's $180 — while still keeping the upside potential if sales are strong. If 40 percent of gross exceeds $150, you get that higher amount; if sales are lighter than expected, you're still guaranteed at least $150 for the day. I'd keep everything else the same: Saturday June 22, 9 to 3, five trays max on the raised benches with all heavy items off the floor for the full duration including setup and teardown per Jon's point, and two November maintenance visits. I think that's a strong package — guaranteed compensation that's competitive with Maya's flat rate, real upside if the day goes well, and twice the maintenance help for the greenhouse. I'm happy with these as my final terms."}
```

